WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a tiny delivery robot that can carry only flat
parcels.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.920 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You need to send it a box for a present.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.680 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could you flatten the box, send the pieces still joined, and
let the robot rebuild it?

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.392 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will investigate which flat arrangements can become
closed objects.

00:00:16.392 --> 00:00:16.872 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to connect flat nets to
three-dimensional objects.

00:00:16.872 --> 00:00:26.683 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:30.883 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to connect flat nets to
three-dimensional objects.

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:36.526 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:36.526 --> 00:00:39.286 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can match faces and their joins.

00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:42.046 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can explain a valid cube net.

00:00:42.046 --> 00:00:44.726 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can explain why a net fails.

00:00:44.726 --> 00:00:48.236 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:50.876 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about an empty cardboard box.

00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:56.236 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Open its flaps carefully, then cut along selected edges
while keeping the faces connected.

00:00:56.236 --> 00:01:01.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When you lay the cardboard flat, you can see regions that
used to face different directions.

00:01:01.356 --> 00:01:04.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This connected arrangement of faces is called a net.

00:01:04.888 --> 00:01:08.008 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The flat drawing tells us more than the number of faces.

00:01:08.008 --> 00:01:12.008 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It shows the shapes of the faces and which edges remain
joined.

00:01:12.008 --> 00:01:17.248 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A fold changes the direction a face points, but does not
change its shape or size.

00:01:17.248 --> 00:01:20.673 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine folding along a shared edge like a hinge.

00:01:20.673 --> 00:01:24.233 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use six equal paper squares or imagine six tiles.

00:01:24.233 --> 00:01:26.593 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Put four squares in a vertical row.

00:01:26.593 --> 00:01:29.913 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Attach one square on each side of the second square.

00:01:29.913 --> 00:01:31.993 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every join is a whole edge.

00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:36.637 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This cross-shaped arrangement is one possible starting point
for a cube net.

00:01:36.637 --> 00:01:40.077 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose the square where the side arms meet as the base.

00:01:40.077 --> 00:01:43.357 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine the neighbouring squares lifting around it.

00:01:43.357 --> 00:01:49.437 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Will all six squares cover six different faces, or will two
squares try to occupy the same place?

00:01:49.437 --> 00:01:53.822 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make a prediction and prepare a reason before we reveal the
result.

00:01:58.822 --> 00:02:01.222 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This cross does fold into a cube.

00:02:01.222 --> 00:02:02.622 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The four neighbours around the base become four sides.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.422 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The remaining square closes the opposite face.

00:02:04.422 --> 00:02:11.862 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The six squares cover six different positions without
leaving a missing face.

00:02:11.862 --> 00:02:15.894 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Folding is about where the faces finish, not just how the
drawing looks.

00:02:15.894 --> 00:02:16.334 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Label the middle square as the base.

00:02:16.334 --> 00:02:22.654 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The square two steps along the long arm becomes the opposite
face.

00:02:22.654 --> 00:02:25.254 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Squares touching the base become its neighbours.

00:02:25.254 --> 00:02:32.555 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Opposite faces do not share an edge on the finished cube,
even when a flat net makes their relationship hard to
imagine.

00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:35.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each face remains the same square during folding.

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:40.155 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its four side lengths stay equal and its corners remain
right angles.

00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:43.035 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The paper changes direction in space.

00:02:43.035 --> 00:02:50.686 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This is why drawing a smaller square or stretching a face
cannot rescue an incorrect arrangement: that would change
the intended cube.

00:02:50.686 --> 00:02:53.966 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now put four equal squares in a horizontal row.

00:02:53.966 --> 00:02:58.126 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Attach one square above the second square and one below that
same square.

00:02:58.126 --> 00:03:04.006 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture the row wrapping around the four side faces, while
the extra squares close the top and bottom.

00:03:04.006 --> 00:03:07.608 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Turning a net on the page does not create a different solid.

00:03:07.608 --> 00:03:11.528 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our two drawings can look different because the page has
been turned.

00:03:11.528 --> 00:03:16.368 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To decide whether they really have different structures,
match their whole-edge joins.

00:03:16.368 --> 00:03:20.808 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rotation or reflection of a working net still folds to a
cube.

00:03:20.808 --> 00:03:25.549 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The relationships between the faces matter more than its
direction on the table.

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:30.749 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here is another arrangement: two rows of three equal
squares, making a rectangle.

00:03:30.749 --> 00:03:33.229 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It has six squares, just like the cross.

00:03:33.229 --> 00:03:36.629 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is the correct face count enough to guarantee a cube?

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:42.763 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine the folds, and look for faces that would collide or
joins that would prevent a proper corner.

00:03:47.763 --> 00:03:50.643 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The rectangle does not fold into a cube.

00:03:50.643 --> 00:03:54.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
At an interior meeting point, four squares surround the same
flat corner.

00:03:54.843 --> 00:03:58.163 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A cube vertex needs three square faces, not four.

00:03:58.163 --> 00:04:05.896 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trying to fold all these joins cannot produce the required
cube corners without cutting an additional join or causing
overlap.

00:04:05.896 --> 00:04:07.576 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A common mistake is saying, six squares means a cube net.

00:04:07.576 --> 00:04:15.016 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six equal squares are necessary, but they must also be
arranged with the right connections.

00:04:15.016 --> 00:04:18.176 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the faces first, then test where they fold.

00:04:18.176 --> 00:04:23.768 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful explanation identifies the obstruction instead of
simply calling the picture wrong.

00:04:23.768 --> 00:04:27.167 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark one base square and imagine its neighbours lifting.

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.327 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep track of the direction of every new face.

00:04:30.327 --> 00:04:35.047 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If two faces would occupy the same cube position, the
arrangement fails.

00:04:35.047 --> 00:04:41.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If all six different positions are filled and the joins
agree, the arrangement can close into the intended cube.

00:04:41.628 --> 00:04:42.348 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A drawing of the finished cube usually shows only three
faces from one viewpoint.

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:48.268 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The net shows all six.

00:04:48.268 --> 00:04:48.668 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These representations describe the same object in different
ways.

00:04:48.668 --> 00:04:58.995 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the net to account for hidden faces, rather than
assuming an unseen face has disappeared from the solid.

00:04:58.995 --> 00:05:01.715 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Consider a long rectangular carton.

00:05:01.715 --> 00:05:04.875 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its faces come in three matching opposite pairs.

00:05:04.875 --> 00:05:06.955 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
They need not all be squares.

00:05:06.955 --> 00:05:11.635 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A suitable net uses rectangles whose joining edges have
matching lengths.

00:05:11.635 --> 00:05:17.452 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six equal squares would make a cube, so they would not
preserve the dimensions of this longer carton.

00:05:17.452 --> 00:05:20.732 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before folding, compare the two edges that need to meet.

00:05:20.732 --> 00:05:22.452 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their lengths must agree.

00:05:22.452 --> 00:05:23.492 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Also check that a closing face has the right shape to fill
the opening.

00:05:23.492 --> 00:05:33.657 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A face count alone cannot check dimensions, just as it could
not check the square arrangement in our earlier example.

00:05:33.657 --> 00:05:37.977 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw or assemble six equal squares in a connected
arrangement.

00:05:37.977 --> 00:05:41.657 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict whether it will fold before testing a paper model.

00:05:41.657 --> 00:05:45.177 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record your prediction, the actual result and one reason.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:51.493 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If your arrangement fails, change a connection and explain
how that change might remove the problem you discovered.

00:05:51.493 --> 00:05:56.613 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our robot can rebuild a box when the flat net preserves the
correct faces and joins.

00:05:56.613 --> 00:06:02.053 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can now connect a net to its solid, test a cube net and
explain why some arrangements fail.

00:06:02.053 --> 00:06:07.799 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one valid and one invalid example, and convince a
partner using folding evidence.
